// ============================================================
// WEATHER TEST DATA — q: question, o: [options a-d], a: correct index,
// r: rationale, ref: topic + enabling objective.
// Source: NIFE Aviation Weather Practice Tests 1-3 (SG Jan 2025,
// CIN Q-9B-0178), incl. their answer keys & reasoning.
// ============================================================
const TESTS = [
{
  name: "Test 1 \u2014 Easy",
  desc: "Single-concept recall drawn directly from Student Guide language.",
  questions: [
    {q:"Which layer of the atmosphere is adjacent to the Earth's surface and contains nearly all weather?", o:["Stratosphere", "Ionosphere", "Tropopause", "Troposphere"], a:3, r:"Troposphere. SG, The Atmosphere / Troposphere: \"The troposphere is the layer adjacent to the Earth's surface\u2026 nearly all weather occurs here. \" The tropopause (C) is only the transition zone at its top, not a weather layer.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Pure, dry air in the atmosphere is composed of approximately:", o:["21% nitrogen, 78% oxygen, 1% other gases", "50% nitrogen, 50% oxygen", "78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 1% other gases", "90% oxygen, 9% nitrogen, 1% other gases"], a:2, r:"78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, 1% other. SG, Composition: \"Pure, dry air contains 78% nitrogen, 21% oxygen, a 1% mixture of ten other gases. \" Note that water vapor (0\u20135% by volume) is separate from dry air.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Atmospheric (barometric) pressure is best defined as:", o:["The density of the air at a given altitude", "The pressure exerted on a surface by the weight of the column of air directly above it", "The temperature of the air corrected for humidity", "The rate at which pressure changes with distance"], a:1, r:"SG, Atmospheric Pressure: \"Atmospheric (barometric) pressure is the pressure exerted on a surface by the atmosphere due to the weight of the column of air directly above that surface. \" D describes the pressure gradient, not pressure.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.173"},
    {q:"Which two units are used in the United States to measure and report atmospheric pressure?", o:["Inches of mercury and millibars", "Knots and statute miles", "Degrees Celsius and degrees Fahrenheit", "Feet AGL and feet MSL"], a:0, r:"Inches of mercury and millibars. SG, Units of Measurement: \"In the U.S., two units are used to measure and report atmospheric pressure: inches of mercury (in-Hg) and millibars (mb). \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.174"},
    {q:"What is the standard (average) temperature lapse rate of the atmosphere in degrees Celsius per 1,000 feet?", o:["1.0", "2.0", "3.0", "3.57"], a:1, r:"2.0\u00b0 C per 1,000 ft. SG, Lapse Rates: \"The average or standard lapse rate is 2\u00b0 Celsius (3.57\u00b0 Fahrenheit) per 1,000 feet. \" Choice D is the same rate expressed in Fahrenheit \u2014 read the units.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.177"},
    {q:"Standard day conditions at sea level are:", o:["30.00 in-Hg and 59\u00b0 C", "1013.2 in-Hg and 15\u00b0 F", "29.92 in-Hg and 15\u00b0 C", "29.92 mb and 59\u00b0 C"], a:2, r:"29.92 in-Hg and 15\u00b0 C. SG, The Standard Atmosphere: \"standard day conditions at sea level, which are 29.92 in-Hg (1013.2 mb) and 15\u00b0 C (59\u00b0 F). \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.178"},
    {q:"Sea level pressure is:", o:["The atmospheric pressure measured directly at the airfield", "The pressure measured at 18,000 feet MSL", "Always 29.92 in-Hg", "The pressure that would be measured from the existing weather if the station were at mean sea level"], a:3, r:"SG, Station and Sea Level Pressure: \"Sea level pressure is the pressure that would be measured from the existing weather if the station were at mean sea level (MSL). \" Choice A defines station pressure.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.179"},
    {q:"Indicated altitude is:", o:["The altitude read directly from the altimeter", "The height of the aircraft above the terrain directly beneath it", "Pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature", "The height above the standard datum plane"], a:0, r:"SG, Altitudes: \"Indicated altitude is the altitude read directly from the altimeter. \" B is absolute/AGL, C is density altitude, D is pressure altitude.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.181"},
    {q:"The height of an aircraft above the terrain directly beneath it is known as:", o:["MSL / true altitude", "Pressure altitude", "Indicated altitude", "AGL / absolute altitude"], a:3, r:"AGL / absolute altitude. SG, Altitudes: \"Absolute Altitude or AGL is the aircraft's height above the terrain directly beneath the aircraft. \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"A change in pressure of 0.10 in-Hg will change the altimeter reading approximately:", o:["10 feet", "1,000 feet", "500 feet", "100 feet"], a:3, r:"100 feet. SG, Pressure: \"A change in pressure of 0.10 in-Hg will change the altimeter reading 100 feet. \" (The 1,000-ft figure corresponds to a full 1.00 in-Hg.)", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"An air mass is defined as a large body of air that has essentially uniform:", o:["Pressure and stability in a vertical plane", "Density and lapse rate throughout its depth", "Wind speed and wind direction at all altitudes", "Temperature and moisture conditions in a horizontal plane"], a:3, r:"SG, Air Masses: \"An air mass is a large body of air that has essentially uniform temperature and moisture conditions in a horizontal plane. \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.184"},
    {q:"A lapse rate in which the temperature increases with increasing altitude is called:", o:["An isothermal lapse rate", "An adiabatic lapse rate", "A standard lapse rate", "A temperature inversion"], a:3, r:"Temperature inversion. SG, Lapse Rates: \"an inversion is a lapse rate where the temperature increases with increasing altitude. \" An isothermal lapse rate (A) means temperature stays the same.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.294"},
    {q:"Relative humidity is:", o:["The temperature at which saturation occurs", "The difference between air temperature and dew point temperature", "The percent of saturation of the air", "The total weight of water vapor in a column of air"], a:2, r:"SG, Atmospheric Moisture: \"relative humidity (RH), which is the percent of saturation of the air. \" A is dew point; B is dew point depression/spread.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.295"},
    {q:"In the Northern Hemisphere, air circulates:", o:["Clockwise around low pressure and counterclockwise around high pressure", "Clockwise around both highs and lows", "Counterclockwise around both highs and lows", "Counterclockwise around low pressure and clockwise around high pressure"], a:3, r:"SG, Large Scale Wind Patterns: air circulates \"clockwise around high pressure, and counterclockwise around low pressure. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"According to Buys Ballot's Law, if the wind is at your back in the Northern Hemisphere:", o:["Lower pressure will be to your left", "Lower pressure will be to your right", "Higher pressure will be directly ahead", "Higher pressure will be directly behind"], a:0, r:"Lower pressure to your left. SG, Buys Ballot's Law: \"if the wind is at your back, the area of lower pressure will be to your left. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Because of surface friction, surface winds blow across the isobars at an assumed angle of approximately:", o:["15\u00b0", "30\u00b0", "45\u00b0", "90\u00b0"], a:2, r:"45\u00b0 . SG, Surface Winds: \"This angle varies as a result of the type of terrain, but for our purposes, we will assume a 45\u00b0 angle. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.186"},
    {q:"The jet stream is best described as:", o:["A narrow band of strong winds of 50 knots or more near the tropopause", "A steady 20-knot wind found just above the surface", "A vertical current of air produced by surface heating", "A band of stratiform cloud found along a warm front"], a:0, r:"SG, The Jet Stream: \"The jet stream is a narrow band of strong winds of 50 knots or more that meanders vertically and horizontally around the hemisphere, \" reaching a maximum near the tropopause.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"In coastal areas, local winds generally blow:", o:["From land to water both day and night", "From water to land both day and night", "From land to water during the day and from water to land at night", "From water to land during the day and from land to water at night"], a:3, r:"SG, Sea and Land Breezes: the daytime sea breeze blows from water to land; the nighttime land breeze blows from land to water.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.188 / 2.189"},
    {q:"Which cloud produces continuous rain, snow, or ice pellets with a base extending down to about 1,000 feet AGL?", o:["Cirrus", "Cumulonimbus", "Altocumulus", "Nimbostratus"], a:3, r:"Nimbostratus. SG, Special Clouds: \"A special cloud, nimbostratus, produces continuous rain, snow, or ice pellets. The cloud base will extend down to about 1,000 ft AGL. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Low clouds have bases ranging from just above the surface to:", o:["5,000 feet AGL", "20,000 feet AGL", "10,000 feet AGL", "6,500 feet AGL"], a:3, r:"6,500 feet AGL. SG, Types of Clouds: \"Low clouds, ranging from just above the surface to 6500 feet AGL. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Cumulonimbus clouds are:", o:["High clouds composed entirely of ice crystals", "Middle clouds that produce virga only", "Stratiform clouds producing light drizzle", "Thunderstorm clouds with numerous hazards to flight"], a:3, r:"SG, Special Clouds: \"Cumulonimbus clouds are thunderstorm clouds\u2026 an exceedingly dangerous cloud, with numerous hazards to flight. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Precipitation characterized by a sudden beginning and ending with abruptly changing intensity is called:", o:["Continuous", "Intermittent", "Showers", "Steady"], a:2, r:"Showers. SG, Characteristics of Precipitation: \"Showers \u2013 Characterized by a sudden beginning and ending, and abruptly changing intensity and/or sky conditions. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.195"},
    {q:"The four methods of lifting are:", o:["Convergence, frontal, orographic, and thermal", "Convergence, subsidence, orographic, and thermal", "Divergence, frontal, adiabatic, and convective", "Convection, conduction, radiation, and advection"], a:0, r:"SG, Methods of Lifting: \"The four methods of lifting are convergence, frontal, orographic, and thermal. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.199"},
    {q:"Lifted air that is warmer than the surrounding air will continue to rise when the lifting action is removed. This indicates:", o:["A stable condition", "A neutrally stable condition", "An unstable condition", "An isothermal condition"], a:2, r:"Unstable. SG, Atmospheric Stability: \"Lifted air that is warmer than the surrounding air continues to rise when the lifting action is removed because it is less dense \u2014 indicates an unstable condition. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.200"},
    {q:"Stratiform clouds are associated with which flight conditions?", o:["Rough flight and showery precipitation", "Smooth flight and continuous precipitation", "Rough flight and clear icing", "Smooth flight and good visibility"], a:1, r:"SG, Stability and Flight Conditions (Figure 2\u201119): stratiform clouds develop in stable air, which brings smooth flight and continuous (steady) precipitation. Note that stable air also brings poor visibility, so D is wrong.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.194"},
    {q:"A front is:", o:["A line of violent thunderstorms ahead of a low", "Any area of low ceilings and poor visibility", "A band of strong winds near the tropopause", "An area of discontinuity that forms between two contrasting air masses"], a:3, r:"SG, Frontal Systems: \"A front is an area of discontinuity that forms between two contrasting air masses when they are adjacent to each other. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.190"},
    {q:"Turbulence is defined as:", o:["Any irregular or disturbed flow in the atmosphere producing gusts and/or eddies", "A sudden change in wind speed or direction over a short distance", "A localized downward flow of air with velocities over 2,000 fpm", "A current of air moving contrary to the main current"], a:0, r:"SG, Turbulence: \"Turbulence is any irregular or disturbed flow in the atmosphere producing gusts and or eddies. \" B defines wind shear; C defines a microburst; D defines an eddy current.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.202"},
    {q:"Which one of the following is NOT one of the intensities used to report turbulence in a PIREP?", o:["Light", "Trace", "Severe", "Extreme"], a:1, r:"Trace. SG, Figure 3\u20111 / Turbulence Intensities: the four turbulence intensities are light, moderate, severe, extreme. \"Trace\" is an icing intensity, not a turbulence intensity \u2014 this is a classic swap.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.203"},
    {q:"The four types of turbulence, divided according to their causative factors, are:", o:["Thermal, mechanical, frontal, and large-scale wind shear", "Thermal, mechanical, orographic, and convergent", "Light, moderate, severe, and extreme", "Occasional, intermittent, continuous, and clear-air"], a:0, r:"SG, Turbulence: \"The different types of turbulence can be divided according to their causative factors: thermal, mechanical, frontal, and large-scale wind shear. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.205"},
    {q:"Mechanical turbulence can result from wind flowing over which of the following?", o:["Irregular terrain", "Buildings", "Mountains", "All of the above"], a:3, r:"All of the above. SG, Mechanical Turbulence: \"when the air near the surface of the Earth flows over obstructions, such as bluffs, hills, mountains, or buildings. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.207"},
    {q:"The four types of structural icing are:", o:["Clear, rime, mixed, and frost", "Clear, rime, induction, and compressor", "Light, moderate, severe, and trace", "Glaze, hoar, sleet, and graupel"], a:0, r:"SG, Aircraft Icing: \"The types of structural icing are clear, rime, mixed, and frost. \" Induction and compressor icing are engine icing, not structural.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.213"},
    {q:"What conditions are required for the formation of aircraft structural icing?", o:["Freezing temperatures and invisible moisture", "Super-cooled visible water droplets, with free air temperature and aircraft surface temperature below freezing", "Freezing temperatures and strong headwinds", "Freezing temperatures and relative humidity above 75 percent"], a:1, r:"SG, Icing Requirements: \"the atmosphere must have super-cooled visible water droplets. Second, the free air temperature\u2026 and the aircraft's surface temperature must be below freezing. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.214"},
    {q:"Clear icing normally occurs at temperatures between:", o:["0\u00b0 C and \u201310\u00b0 C", "\u201310\u00b0 C and \u201320\u00b0 C", "\u20138\u00b0 C and \u201315\u00b0 C", "\u201320\u00b0 C and \u201340\u00b0 C"], a:0, r:"0\u00b0 C to \u201310\u00b0 C. SG, Structural Icing Conditions: \"Clear icing normally occurs at temperatures between 0\u00b0 C and \u201310\u00b0 C. \" (\u201310 to \u201320 is rime; \u20138 to \u201315 is mixed.)", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.215"},
    {q:"An aerodynamic hazard associated with structural icing is that it:", o:["Reduces lift and increases stall speed", "Increases lift and decreases stall speed", "Decreases drag and decreases weight", "Has no effect until at least one inch has accumulated"], a:0, r:"SG, Effects and Hazards of Structural Icing: ice \"decreases lift, thrust, and range, and increases drag, weight, fuel consumption, and stall speed. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.216"},
    {q:"Engine failure due to icing in a jet aircraft is generally the result of:", o:["Carburetor icing", "Structural icing on the wings", "Induction icing", "Frost on the empennage"], a:2, r:"Induction icing. SG, Other Types of Aircraft Icing: induction icing forms in the air intake duct; the reduced pressure at the intake lowers the temperature enough for condensation/deposition. Carburetor icing (A) does not apply to a jet.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.217"},
    {q:"Fog is a visible aggregate of minute water droplets based at or within 50 feet of the surface that reduces prevailing visibility to less than:", o:["1 statute mile", "3 statute miles", "\u215d statute mile", "7 statute miles"], a:2, r:"\u215d statute mile. SG, Fog vs Stratus: fog \"is based at or within 50 feet of the surface, is greater than 20 feet in depth, and reduces the prevailing visibility to less than \u215d of a statute mile. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.219"},
    {q:"Which type of fog forms when warm, moist air moves over a colder surface?", o:["Radiation fog", "Advection fog", "Valley fog", "Steam fog"], a:1, r:"Advection fog. SG, Advection Fog: \"Advection fog occurs when warm, moist air moves over a cold surface and the air is cooled to the dew point. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.222"},
    {q:"The basic requirements for thunderstorm formation are:", o:["Moisture, stable air, a lifting action, and buildup through the freezing layer", "Moisture, unstable air, a lifting action, and buildup through the freezing layer", "Dry air, subsidence, and a temperature inversion", "High pressure, light winds, and clear skies"], a:1, r:"SG, Thunderstorm Development: \"The basic requirements for thunderstorm formation are moisture, unstable air, some type of lifting action, and building up through the freezing layer. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"A microburst is:", o:["An intense, highly localized downward atmospheric flow", "A rotating column of air descending from a cumulonimbus cloud", "A line of violent thunderstorms ahead of a cold front", "The arc-shaped leading edge of downdraft air ahead of a storm"], a:0, r:"SG, Microbursts: \"A microburst is an intense, highly localized downward atmospheric flow with velocities of 2000 to over 6000 feet per minute. \" B is a tornado, C is a squall line, D is a gust front.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"Prevailing visibility is:", o:["The distance on final approach at which the runway environment is in sight", "The greatest horizontal visibility equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle", "The average forward horizontal distance measured from the cockpit in flight", "The horizontal distance a pilot will see looking down the runway from the approach end"], a:1, r:"SG, Visibility Definitions: \"Prevailing Visibility \u2013 The greatest horizontal visibility, measured in statute miles, equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"Runway Visual Range (RVR) is:", o:["The distance on final approach at which the runway environment is in sight", "The greatest horizontal visibility equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle", "The horizontal distance a pilot will see by looking down the runway from the approach end", "The vertical distance seen upward into an obscuration"], a:2, r:"SG, Visibility Definitions: \"Runway Visual Range (RVR) \u2013 The horizontal distance\u2026 a pilot will see by looking down the runway from the approach end. \" A is slant range visibility.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"A sky coverage of 5/8 through 7/8 is reported as:", o:["FEW", "SCT", "BKN", "OVC"], a:2, r:"BKN. SG, Figure 4\u20113 / Figure 4\u201120: FEW = 1/8\u20132/8, SCT = 3/8\u20134/8, BKN = 5/8\u20137/8, OVC = 8/8.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"A ceiling is the height AGL ascribed to:", o:["The lowest few or scattered layer", "The lowest broken or overcast layer, or the vertical visibility into a total obscuration", "The highest overcast layer reported", "The top of the lowest cloud layer"], a:1, r:"SG, Sky Coverage and Ceilings: \"A ceiling is the height above the ground (AGL) ascribed to the lowest broken or overcast layer; or the vertical visibility into an obscuring phenomenon (total obscuration). \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"The information displayed on a Surface Analysis Chart is:", o:["Observed weather, representing past history", "A forecast valid for the next 24 hours", "A forecast valid for up to one week", "Winds and temperatures aloft only"], a:0, r:"SG, Surface Analysis: \"The information displayed on the Surface Analysis Chart is observed weather, meaning that the chart represents past history, and is not a forecast. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.18"},
    {q:"Prognostic (Prog) Charts provide:", o:["Only the current observed surface analysis", "The latest surface analysis along with multiple forecasts for up to a week", "Radar returns from the previous hour", "Satellite imagery in the infrared spectrum"], a:1, r:"SG, Prognostic Charts: to a week. \" \"These charts include the latest surface analysis along with multiple forecasts for up", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.20"},
    {q:"In a METAR, the group \"27004KT\" indicates:", o:["Wind from 270 degrees at 4 knots", "Wind toward 270 degrees at 4 knots", "Wind from 004 degrees at 27 knots", "Wind from 270 degrees at 40 knots"], a:0, r:"SG, Group 4: Wind: direction is given first in three digits (270\u00b0), speed in the following digits (04 kt), and wind is always reported as the direction it blows FROM.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"In a METAR, the group \"A2997\" indicates:", o:["An altimeter setting of 2,997 millibars", "An altimeter setting of 29.97 inches of mercury", "A sea level pressure of 999.7 millibars", "A station pressure of 29.97 millibars"], a:1, r:"SG, Group 10: Altimeter Setting: \"The altimeter group always starts with the letter \u2018A' , and will be followed with a 4-digit group using the tens, units, tenths, and hundredths of inches of mercury. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"A Terminal Aerodrome Forecast (TAF) provides:", o:["The latest observed weather at a station", "Forecast surface weather conditions for an airfield", "A depiction of pressure centers and fronts", "Winds and temperatures aloft"], a:1, r:"SG, The Terminal Aerodrome Forecast (TAF): the METAR provides existing weather; the TAF provides \"the surface forecasted weather conditions. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.24"},
    {q:"On ground-based weather radar, the strongest return signals are yielded by:", o:["Snow", "Small water droplets", "Large drops of water and hail", "Clear air between cells"], a:2, r:"SG, RADAR information: \"The large drops of water and hail, if present, within thunderstorms yield the strongest return signals\u2026 snow produces the faintest echo. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.28"},
    {q:"The National Weather Service defines a severe thunderstorm as one producing hail at least ___ diameter, and/or wind gusts of ___ or greater, and/or a tornado.", o:["1 inch; 58 mph", "2 inches; 75 mph", "\u00bd inch; 35 mph", "\u00be inch; 50 mph"], a:0, r:"1 inch hail; 58 mph winds. SG, Severe Weather Watch Bulletins: \"A severe thunderstorm refers to a thunderstorm producing hail that is at least 1 inch in diameter or larger, and/or wind gusts of 58 mph or greater, and/or a tornado. \" Choice B is the significant severe threshold.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.30"},
  ]
},
{
  name: "Test 2 \u2014 Medium",
  desc: "Applied relationships, decode drills, and plausible near-miss distractors.",
  questions: [
    {q:"Which one of the following correctly states the height of the troposphere?", o:["28,000 ft MSL over the equator, 55,000 ft MSL over the poles, 36,000 ft MSL average over the U.S.", "53,000 ft MSL over the equator, 36,000 ft MSL over the poles, 28,000 ft MSL average over the U.S.", "36,000 ft MSL over the equator, 18,000 ft MSL over the poles, 53,000 ft MSL average over the U.S.", "55,000 ft MSL over the equator, 28,000 ft MSL over the poles, 36,000 ft MSL average over the U.S."], a:3, r:"SG, Troposphere: \"It varies in height from an average 55,000 feet MSL over the equator to 28,000 feet MSL over the poles. The average height of the troposphere over the United States is 36,000 feet MSL. \" Choice A is the same numbers with the equator and poles reversed \u2014 the classic trap.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Approximately 50% of the atmosphere by weight lies below , and 90% lies within . ______ ______", o:["18,000 feet; 53,000 feet", "18,000 feet; 36,000 feet", "36,000 feet; 55,000 feet", "28,000 feet; 55,000 feet"], a:0, r:"18,000 ft / 53,000 ft. SG, Troposphere: \"roughly 50% of it, by weight, lies below 18,000 feet and 90% within 53,000 feet. \" Note 53,000 ft is not the same as the 55,000 ft equatorial troposphere height in Q1.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Which statement about the tropopause is correct?", o:["Temperature decreases rapidly with altitude, and the weakest winds are found there", "Temperature increases with altitude, and it is where nearly all weather occurs", "Temperature is constant with altitude change, and the strongest winds occur just below it", "Temperature is constant with altitude change, and the strongest winds occur just above it"], a:2, r:"SG, Troposphere / tropopause: \"The temperature in this layer is constant with altitude change\u2026 the strongest winds, those of the jet stream, occur just below the tropopause. \" D is correct on temperature but wrong on where the jet stream sits.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.172"},
    {q:"Isobars that are closely spaced on a surface analysis chart indicate:", o:["A shallow pressure gradient and slower wind speeds", "A steep pressure gradient and slower wind speeds", "A shallow pressure gradient and faster wind speeds", "A steep pressure gradient and faster wind speeds"], a:3, r:"SG, Pressure Gradients: \"The PGF is steep, or strong, when isobars are close together\u2026 the steeper the gradient, the stronger the winds. \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.175"},
    {q:"In the lower atmosphere, a 1,000-foot increase in altitude results in a pressure decrease of approximately:", o:["1 in-Hg (34 mb)", "0.10 in-Hg (3.4 mb)", "2 in-Hg (68 mb)", "1 mb (0.03 in-Hg)"], a:0, r:"1 in-Hg (34 mb). SG, The Standard Atmosphere: \"a 1,000 foot increase in altitude will result in a pressure decrease of approximately 1 in-Hg (34 mb). \" Choice B is the 0.10 in-Hg = 100 ft relationship.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.178"},
    {q:"A station is located at 2,500 feet MSL and is reporting a sea level pressure of 1045.0 mb. Assuming the standard pressure lapse rate, what is the station pressure?", o:["960.0 mb", "1045.0 mb", "1047.0 mb", "1130.0 mb"], a:0, r:"960.0 mb. Field elevation is 2,500 ft; at 34 mb per 1,000 ft that is a correction of 85 mb. Sea level pressure = station pressure + correction, so station pressure = 1045.0 \u2013 85 = 960.0 mb. Pressure always decreases with altitude, so the station's own pressure must be lower than the sea level value \u2014 which eliminates C and D immediately. (Note: one of the legacy practice tests keys this question as 1130.0 mb. That answer is inconsistent with the SG and with the physics; 960 is correct.)", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.179"},
    {q:"Using the standard lapse rate, a pilot at 10,000 feet MSL with an outside air temperature of \u20138\u00b0 C should do what to reach an altitude where the temperature is +4\u00b0 C?", o:["Descend to approximately 2,000 feet MSL", "Climb to find an inversion", "Descend to approximately 6,000 feet MSL", "Descend to approximately 4,000 feet MSL"], a:3, r:"Descend to approximately 4,000 ft MSL. A change from \u20138\u00b0 C to +4\u00b0 C is 12\u00b0 C. At 2\u00b0 C per 1,000 ft that is 6,000 feet of descent: 10,000 \u2013 6,000 = 4,000 ft MSL. Choice C is the amount of the descent, not the resulting altitude.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.177"},
    {q:"Which type of altitude is required in the United States above 18,000 feet MSL, and what altimeter setting corresponds to it?", o:["True altitude; local altimeter setting", "Absolute altitude; local altimeter setting", "Pressure altitude; 29.92 in-Hg", "Density altitude; 29.92 in-Hg"], a:2, r:"SG, Altitudes: \"Federal Aviation Rules (FAR) require that all aircraft operating above 18,000 feet MSL set 29.92 in to the altimeter. \" That is by definition a pressure altitude.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"An aircraft at Sherman Field (elevation 28 ft MSL) shows an indicated altitude of 70 feet with the local altimeter setting in the Kollsman window. This aircraft:", o:["Has an instrument error of +42 feet and is legal for instrument flight", "Has an instrument error of +98 feet and is not legal for instrument flight", "Has an instrument error of \u201342 feet and is not legal for instrument flight", "Has no instrument error; the difference is caused by nonstandard temperature"], a:0, r:"SG, Altitudes: instrument error is the difference between field elevation and indicated altitude \u2014 70 \u2013 28 = +42 ft. \"If the instrument error is in excess of 75 ft, the aircraft is considered unsafe for instrument flight, \" so +42 ft is within limits.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.181"},
    {q:"An increase in density altitude will:", o:["Shorten takeoff distance and increase rate of climb", "Lengthen takeoff distance and increase rate of climb", "Shorten takeoff distance and reduce rate of climb", "Lengthen takeoff distance and reduce rate of climb"], a:3, r:"SG, Density altitude: rate of climb. \" \"higher density altitudes result in longer takeoff and landing distances and a reduced", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"The altimeter setting at Randolph AFB is 29.85 in-Hg; at Vance AFB it is 30.15 in-Hg. A pilot sets the altimeter correctly at Randolph and flies to Vance at an indicated altitude of 5,000 feet without resetting. Assuming a standard lapse rate, the true (MSL) altitude over Vance is:", o:["4,700 feet", "5,000 feet", "5,030 feet", "5,300 feet"], a:3, r:"5,300 feet. This is a low-to-high pressure change (\"Low to High, Plenty of Sky\"), so the aircraft is higher than indicated. Altitude error = (30.15 \u2013 29.85) \u00d7 1,000 ft/in-Hg = 300 ft. MSL = assigned altitude + altitude error = 5,000 + 300 = 5,300 ft. Choice A applies the error in the wrong direction; choice C uses 0.10 in-Hg = 10 ft.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"Cold air masses are considered because the air is than the surface it moves over. ______ ______", o:["stable; colder", "unstable; warmer", "stable; warmer", "unstable; colder"], a:3, r:"unstable; colder. SG, Air Masses: \"cold air masses are inherently unstable. This is because cold air masses typically come from the north and consist of colder air moving over warmer surfaces. \" The air is colder than the surface beneath it, so it is heated from below \u2014 the classic recipe for instability. (Warm air masses are the reverse: warmer than the surface, therefore stable.) This matches the SG's own review question 5\u20111\u20113 #19.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.184"},
    {q:"Once the dew point spread reaches approximately 2\u00b0 C (4\u00b0 F), the relative humidity will be about ______ and water vapor will begin to condense into fog or clouds.", o:["100%", "50%", "75%", "90%"], a:3, r:"90%. SG, Atmospheric Moisture: \"Once the dew point spread reaches about 2\u00b0 C / 4\u00b0 F, the RH will be 90%, and the water vapor will begin to condense into fog or clouds. \" 100% RH corresponds to a spread of zero.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.197 / 2.198"},
    {q:"Gradient winds:", o:["Flow parallel to the isobars and are found above 2,000 feet AGL", "Flow parallel to the isobars and are found below 2,000 feet AGL", "Flow across the isobars at 45\u00b0 and are found above 2,000 feet AGL", "Flow perpendicular to the isobars at all altitudes"], a:0, r:"SG, Gradient Winds: gradient winds \"flow parallel to the isobars\u2026 gradient winds are found above 2,000 feet AGL. \" Below 2,000 ft AGL friction produces the 45\u00b0 cross-isobar surface wind.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly pairs a force with its role in producing wind?", o:["The Coriolis force is the initiating force for all winds", "Friction is the initiating force for all winds", "The pressure gradient force is the initiating force for all winds, and the Coriolis force deflects the air to the right in the Northern Hemisphere", "The pressure gradient force deflects the air to the right, and the Coriolis force initiates all winds"], a:2, r:"SG, Pressure Gradients / Gradient Winds: \"The PGF is the initiating force for all winds, \" and \"The Coriolis force, created by the Earth's rotation, diverts the air to the right. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Jet stream winds average about knots, but may reach speeds in excess of ______ ______ knots.", o:["50 to 100; 200", "200 to 250; 350", "150 to 200; 300", "100 to 150; 250"], a:3, r:"100 to 150 kt average, in excess of 250 kt. SG, The Jet Stream: \"These winds average about 100\u2011150 knots but may reach speeds in excess of 250 knots. \" (50 kt is the minimum threshold that defines a jet stream, not the average.)", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"Jet streams are most frequently found in segments of:", o:["100 to 200 miles in length, 10 to 50 miles wide, 2,000 to 10,000 feet in height", "1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 10 to 40 miles wide, 30,000 to 40,000 feet in height", "500 to 1,000 miles in length, 50 to 100 miles wide, 5,000 to 15,000 feet in height", "1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 100 to 400 miles wide, 3,000 to 7,000 feet in height"], a:3, r:"SG, The Jet Stream: \"found in segments from 1,000 to 3,000 miles in length, 100 to 400 miles in depth/width, and 3,000 to 7,000 feet in height. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"Which statement about land and sea breezes is correct?", o:["Land breezes are usually stronger than sea breezes and penetrate far inland", "Both are deep in vertical extent and are unaffected by season", "Sea breezes are usually stronger than land breezes, but seldom penetrate far inland", "Sea breezes blow offshore during the day and reach 15 to 20 knots"], a:2, r:"SG, Sea and Land Breezes: \"The sea breezes are usually stronger than the land breezes, but they seldom penetrate far inland. \" D reverses the sea breeze direction \u2014 a sea breeze is an onshore wind.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.188 / 2.189"},
    {q:"Middle clouds have bases between ______ and carry the prefix . ______", o:["6,500 and 20,000 ft AGL; alto-", "5,000 and 18,500 ft AGL; alto-", "6,500 and 20,000 ft AGL; cirro-", "6,500 and 20,000 ft MSL; nimbo-"], a:0, r:"SG, Middle Clouds: \"cloud bases form between 6,500 and 20,000 feet AGL. The names of the middle clouds will contain the prefix alto- . \" Cloud heights in this course are always AGL, which eliminates D; cirro- is the high cloud prefix.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"A pilot approaching an active warm front from the east would encounter the cloud sequence:", o:["Stratus, nimbostratus, altostratus, cirrostratus, cirrus", "Altocumulus, cumulonimbus, fair weather cumulus", "Cumulus, towering cumulus, cumulonimbus, anvil cirrus", "Cirrus, cirrostratus, altostratus, nimbostratus, stratus"], a:3, r:"SG, Recognizing Warm Fronts During Flight: \"If one were to approach the front from the east, the sequence of clouds would be cirrus, cirrostratus, altostratus, nimbostratus, and stratus, rain and fog. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"On average, a cold front moves toward the at knots, with a wind shift from . ______ ______ ______", o:["NE; 15; SE to SW", "NE; 20; SE to NW", "SE; 15; SW to NW", "SE; 20; SW to NW"], a:3, r:"SE at 20 kt, wind shift SW to NW. SG, Cold Fronts: \"Cold fronts move toward the SE at 20 kts, on average, and the wind shift is from the SW to the NW. \" Choice A is the warm front (NE at 15 kt, SE to SW).", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"A stationary front is characterized by:", o:["A 90\u00b0 wind shift from SE to SW and movement toward the NE at 15 knots", "A 90\u00b0 wind shift from SW to NW and rapidly clearing postfrontal skies", "A 180\u00b0 wind shift and movement toward the NE at the speed of the front remaining on the ground", "A 180\u00b0 wind shift and little or no movement, with weather similar to but less intense than a warm front"], a:3, r:"SG, Stationary Fronts: \"a stationary front has a 180\u00b0 wind shift\" ; \"The weather conditions occurring with a stationary front are similar to those found with the warm front, but are usually less intense. \" Choice C describes an occlusion.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Occluded fronts are depicted on surface analysis charts in ______ and generally move toward the . ______", o:["purple; NE", "purple; SE", "red and blue alternating; NE", "blue; SE"], a:0, r:"Purple; NE. SG, Occluded Fronts: \"Occlusions are shown on surface charts with both cold and warm frontal symbols pointing in the same direction, but colored purple. Both types of occlusions tend to be aligned from NW to SE, and hence move toward the NE. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"A squall line is a line of violent thunderstorms that typically develops:", o:["5 to 20 miles ahead of a cold front and perpendicular to it", "50 to 300 miles ahead of a cold front and roughly parallel to it", "500 to 700 miles ahead of a warm front and roughly parallel to it", "Directly along the surface position of a warm front"], a:1, r:"SG, Squall Lines: \"They develop 50 to 300 miles ahead of the cold front and roughly parallel to it. \" Choice A is the gust front distance (5\u201320 miles); choice C is the extent of the warm front cloud shield.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.193"},
    {q:"Which set of properties is used to locate and classify fronts?", o:["Temperature, moisture (dew point), winds, and pressure", "Temperature, altitude, density, and stability", "Ceiling, visibility, precipitation, and icing", "Isotachs, isotherms, isogonic lines, and contour lines"], a:0, r:"SG, Frontal Discontinuities: \"Differences in the various properties of adjacent air masses\u2013in particular, their temperature, moisture (indicated by the dew point), winds, and pressure\u2013are used to locate and classify fronts. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.191"},
    {q:"Which grouping correctly lists \"signs in the sky\" that indicate a stable atmosphere?", o:["Thunderstorms, showers, towering clouds, dust devils", "Temperature inversions, low fog and stratus, rising air temperature while climbing", "Rapidly decreasing air temperature while climbing, cumuliform clouds, gusty winds", "Good visibility, showery precipitation, clear icing"], a:1, r:"SG, Atmospheric Stability: \"Here are some additional \u2018signs in the sky' that indicate stable air: temperature inversions, low fog and stratus, and rising air temperature while climbing. \" Choices A and C list the unstable indicators; D mixes unstable indicators in.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.194 / 2.200"},
    {q:"A pilot encountering turbulence between one-third and two-thirds of the time would report the turbulence as:", o:["Occasional", "Continuous", "Intermittent", "Moderate"], a:2, r:"Intermittent. SG, Figure 3\u20111 / Turbulence Intensities and Reporting: occasional = less than \u2153 of the time; intermittent = \u2153 to \u2154; continuous = more than \u2154. Note that \"moderate\" (D) is an intensity, not a time descriptor.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.204"},
    {q:"Clear air turbulence (CAT) normally occurs:", o:["Below 15,000 feet MSL and is caused by surface heating", "Above 15,000 feet MSL and is caused by strong wind shears in the jet stream", "Above 15,000 feet MSL and is caused exclusively by mountain waves", "Only within cumulonimbus clouds"], a:1, r:"SG, Clear Air Turbulence: \"CAT normally occurs outside of clouds and usually occurs at altitudes above 15,000 feet MSL, due to strong wind shears in the jet stream. \" C is too absolute \u2014 CAT can occur in all four turbulence categories.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.211"},
    {q:"The strength of thermal (convective) turbulence depends in part on the extent to which the Earth's surface has been heated, which in turn depends on the nature of the surface. Which surface would produce the strongest convective currents on a clear, sunny day?", o:["A plowed field", "A grassy field", "A forested area", "A lake"], a:0, r:"Plowed field. SG, Thermal Turbulence: \"dry, barren surfaces such as sandy or rocky wasteland and plowed fields absorb heat more readily than surfaces covered with grass or other vegetation\u2026 Thus, barren surfaces generally cause stronger convective currents. \" Water surfaces heat most slowly.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.206"},
    {q:"The strength and vertical extent of mechanical turbulence depends on wind speed, terrain roughness, and air stability. Which of these appears to be the most important factor?", o:["Wind speed", "Terrain roughness", "Stability of the air", "Air temperature"], a:2, r:"Stability of the air. SG, Mechanical Turbulence: \"Stability seems to be the most important factor in determining the strength and vertical extent of the mechanical turbulence. \" Wind speed and terrain roughness are contributing factors, but the SG singles out stability.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.207"},
    {q:"The most severe cases of frontal turbulence are generally associated with:", o:["Fast-moving warm fronts", "Slow-moving cold fronts", "Fast-moving cold fronts", "Stationary fronts"], a:2, r:"SG, Frontal Turbulence: \"The most severe cases of frontal turbulence are generally associated with fast moving cold fronts. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.208"},
    {q:"When taking off or landing in near-calm surface winds under clear skies within a few hours of sunrise, a pilot should expect a shear zone at an inversion if the wind at 2,000 to 4,000 feet AGL is:", o:["10 knots or more", "15 knots or more", "25 knots or more", "50 knots or more"], a:2, r:"25 knots or more. SG, Temperature Inversions: \"If the wind at 2000 to 4000 feet AGL is 25 knots or more, expect a shear zone at the inversion. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.209"},
    {q:"Which one of the following is NOT a recommended procedure for flying through turbulence?", o:["Establish and maintain thrust settings consistent with turbulent air penetration airspeed", "Control pitch and bank by reference to the attitude gyro indicator", "Chase the altimeter to hold assigned altitude precisely", "Use moderate control inputs to reestablish the desired attitude"], a:2, r:"SG, Flight Techniques for Turbulence: \"Allow altitude to vary. Sacrifice altitude to maintain desired attitude. Don't chase the altimeter. \" The other three are verbatim recommendations.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.212"},
    {q:"Rime icing is best described as:", o:["Milky white, forming from small droplets in stable clouds at \u201310\u00b0 to \u201320\u00b0 C", "Glossy and transparent, forming from large droplets in unstable clouds at 0\u00b0 to \u201310\u00b0 C", "Lumpy but hard and dense, forming at \u20138\u00b0 to \u201315\u00b0 C", "A thin layer of crystalline ice formed by deposition on a parked aircraft"], a:0, r:"SG, Structural Icing Conditions: \"Rime icing is milky white in appearance and is most likely to occur at temperatures of \u201310 to \u201320\u00b0 C\u2026 Rime ice occurs in stable conditions\u2013clouds where the water droplets are small. \" B describes clear ice, C describes mixed, D describes frost.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.213 / 2.215"},
    {q:"The most frequently encountered type of aircraft icing is:", o:["Clear ice", "Rime ice", "Mixed ice", "Frost"], a:2, r:"Mixed ice. SG, Structural Icing Conditions: \"The most frequent type of icing encountered is usually a form of mixed icing. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.213"},
    {q:"A pilot encounters clear icing in freezing precipitation below the clouds of a warm front. The best course of action is generally to:", o:["Continue on the current flight path until clear of the precipitation", "Descend, regardless of the minimum en route altitude", "Climb to warmer temperatures across the frontal boundary", "Fly parallel to the front until out of the icing"], a:2, r:"SG, Minimizing or Avoiding Icing Hazards: \"if encountering clear icing in the freezing precipitation below the clouds of a warm front, the aircraft is most likely in the cold air ahead of the warm front. In this case, the best alternative may be to climb to warmer temperatures, across the frontal boundary, as the freezing precipitation may extend all the way to ground level. \" Choice D is explicitly prohibited (\"Do not fly parallel to a front while encountering icing conditions\").", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.218"},
    {q:"Which set correctly lists the three conditions required for fog to form?", o:["Condensation nuclei, a high water content (low dew point spread), and light surface winds", "Condensation nuclei, a large dew point spread, and calm winds", "High water content, freezing temperatures, and clear skies", "Condensation nuclei, unstable air, and a lifting action"], a:0, r:"SG, Fog Formation: \"For fog to form, three conditions must be satisfied: (1) condensation nuclei must be present in the air, (2) the air must have a high water content (a low dew point spread), (3) and light surface winds must be present. \" Choice D lists thunderstorm requirements.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.221"},
    {q:"With respect to radiation fog, winds of 5 to 10 knots will usually:", o:["Result in shallow fog", "Dissipate the fog and cause low stratus to form", "Cause dense fog", "Have no effect on fog formation"], a:2, r:"Cause dense fog. SG, Radiation Fog: \"Winds less than 5 knots usually results in shallow fog. Winds of 5 to 10 knots will usually cause dense fog. Winds of greater than 10 knots will usually dissipate the fog. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.222"},
    {q:"When it is necessary to fly over the top of a thunderstorm, the recommended altitude separation from the cloud tops is:", o:["1,000 feet above the tops", "100 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops", "500 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops", "1,000 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops"], a:3, r:"SG, Over the Top: \"allow a margin of safety by choosing an altitude separation from the top of the thunderstorm of 1000 feet for every 10 knots of wind speed at the altitude of the tops. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.225"},
    {q:"Flight visibility is:", o:["The greatest horizontal visibility equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle", "The average forward horizontal distance, measured from the cockpit of an aircraft in flight, at which a pilot can see and identify prominent unlighted objects by day and prominent lighted objects at night", "The distance on final approach at which the runway environment is in sight", "The distance seen down the runway from the approach end"], a:1, r:"SG, Visibility Definitions: \"Flight Visibility \u2013 The average forward horizontal distance, measured in statute miles from the cockpit of an aircraft in flight, at which a pilot can see and identify prominent unlighted objects by day and prominent lighted objects at night. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"A report of \"SCT000\" in a METAR indicates:", o:["A ceiling of zero feet", "A partial obscuration, which does not constitute a ceiling", "A total obscuration with vertical visibility of zero", "Scattered clouds with bases at the standard datum plane"], a:1, r:"SG, Sky Coverage and Ceilings and Group 8: Sky Condition: a surface-based obscuration reported as FEW000/SCT000/BKN000 is a partial obscuration, and \"partial obscurations are not considered ceilings. \" A total obscuration would be coded VV.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"Pilots are required to submit a PIREP in which of the following situations?", o:["Only when specifically requested by ATC", "When weather on an IFR approach differs from the latest observation, when wind shear is encountered on departure or arrival, when requested by ATC, when unusual or unforeseen weather is encountered, and when a missed approach is executed", "Only after landing, and only if the weather was worse than forecast", "Whenever any cloud layer is observed at or below 10,000 feet"], a:1, r:"SG, Pilot Weather Reports: the five required reporting conditions are listed verbatim.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.228"},
    {q:"A non-convective U.S. SIGMET is issued for which of the following?", o:["Moderate turbulence, moderate icing, and IFR conditions", "Severe icing, severe or extreme turbulence, dust/sandstorms lowering visibility below 3 miles, and volcanic ash", "Thunderstorms only", "Sustained surface winds greater than 30 knots and low-level wind shear"], a:1, r:"SG, SIGMETs: US (non-convective) SIGMETs are issued for \"Severe Icing; Severe or Extreme Turbulence; Dust Storms and/or Sand Storms lowering visibility to less than 3 miles; Volcanic Ash. \" Choice A lists AIRMET criteria; choice D lists specific G\u2011AIRMET (TANGO) items.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.229"},
    {q:"On a station model, the sea level pressure is depicted as \"985. \" Decoded in millibars, this is:", o:["985.0 mb", "998.5 mb", "1098.5 mb", "1985 mb"], a:1, r:"998.5 mb. SG, Station Model Plots: for millibars the hundreds/thousands digit and the decimal point are dropped. \"Pressures beginning with large numbers (such as a 9) really start with a hidden \u20189' , \" so 985 \u2192 998.5 mb. A value beginning with 1 or 0 would take a hidden \"10. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.21"},
    {q:"A METAR reports \"1/2SM FG VV004. \" This indicates:", o:["A cloud ceiling of 400 feet AGL with fog beneath it", "An indefinite ceiling with a vertical visibility of 400 feet into the fog", "A partial obscuration with 4/8 sky coverage", "Clouds at 4,000 feet AGL and fog at the surface"], a:1, r:"SG, Sky Coverage and Ceilings / Group 8: \"When the sky is totally obscured by a surface-based obscuration the only group in the sky condition section will be a 5-digit group, the first 2 digits VV\u2026 and the last 3 digits the height of the vertical visibility into the indefinite ceiling. \" VV004 = 400 ft. This is not the same as a 400-ft cloud ceiling, because the obscuration also degrades slant range visibility.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"RVR is reported whenever:", o:["Prevailing visibility is 3 statute miles or less", "Prevailing visibility is 1 statute mile or less, and/or the RVR for the designated instrument runway is 6,000 feet or less", "The ceiling is at or below 1,000 feet", "Any obscuring phenomenon is present"], a:1, r:"SG, Group 6: Runway Visual Range: \"RVR is reported whenever the prevailing visibility is 1 statute mile or less and/or the RVR for the designated instrument runway is 6000 feet or less. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"In a U.S. military TAF, a visibility code of \"9999\" indicates:", o:["Visibility of 9,999 meters exactly", "Visibility of 7 miles or greater (unlimited)", "Visibility is not forecast", "Visibility of 9,999 feet"], a:1, r:"SG, Visibility, Weather, and Obstructions to Vision: \"A visibility code of \u20189999' indicates 7 miles visibility or greater is forecast, i.e. unlimited visibility. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes the TAF change groups?", o:["FM indicates a rapid, permanent change; BECMG indicates a slow, permanent change; TEMPO indicates a temporary, non-permanent change", "FM indicates a slow change; BECMG indicates a rapid change; TEMPO indicates a permanent change", "All three indicate permanent changes and differ only in duration", "TEMPO supersedes all previously forecast conditions for the remainder of the TAF"], a:0, r:"SG, Change Group Terminology: \"FM is used when the change is expected to be quick, and BECMG is used when the change is expected to occur over a longer period of time\u2026 TEMPO (Temporary) means just that: a temporary or non-permanent change. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"On a Winds-Aloft Forecast, the code \"9900\" indicates:", o:["Winds from 099\u00b0 at 00 knots", "Winds light and variable", "Winds from 990\u00b0 \u2014 a coding error", "Winds from 090\u00b0 at 100 knots"], a:1, r:"Light and variable. SG, Special Circumstances 1: \"When forecast wind speeds are less than 5 knots, direction is difficult to determine, and the winds are called \u2018light and variable, ' and the code \u20189900' will be listed. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"For flight planning purposes, an aviator must consider the worst weather conditions falling within which window?", o:["From 1 hour prior to ETA up to but not including 1 hour after ETA", "From 30 minutes prior to ETA up to 30 minutes after ETA", "From ETA to the end of the TAF period", "The entire 24-hour TAF period"], a:0, r:"SG, Using TAFs For Flight Planning: \"an aviator must consider the worst weather conditions that fall within the period of 1 hour prior to the planned estimated time of arrival (ETA) up to but not including 1 hour after ETA, for a total of a 2-hour window. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25 / 4.32"},
  ]
},
{
  name: "Test 3 \u2014 Hard",
  desc: "Near-miss distractors, NOT-style stems, and applied scenarios. Read every option.",
  questions: [
    {q:"Which one of the following statements is NOT correct?", o:["Pressure always decreases with altitude", "In the lower atmosphere, pressure decreases more rapidly with altitude than it does at higher altitudes", "Temperature always decreases with altitude within the troposphere", "Close to the surface, the measured lapse rate may show an increase, a decrease, or no change in temperature"], a:2, r:"(the incorrect statement). SG, Troposphere: \"Within the troposphere, the temperature normally decreases with increasing altitude\" \u2014 and Lapse Rates: \"close to the surface of the earth the lapse rate may indicate an increase, decrease, or a constant temperature. \" Inversions and isothermal layers exist inside the troposphere, so \"always\" makes B false. A, C, and D are all verbatim SG statements. Pressure, unlike temperature, always decreases with altitude \u2014 that is the asymmetry this question tests.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.173 / 2.176"},
    {q:"Which one of the following correctly lists standard day sea level pressure, temperature, pressure lapse rate, and temperature lapse rate?", o:["29.92 in-Hg, 15\u00b0 C, 1 in-Hg/1,000 ft, 2\u00b0 C/1,000 ft", "29.92 in-Hg, 59\u00b0 C, 1 in-Hg/1,000 ft, 2\u00b0 C/1,000 ft", "29.92 in-Hg, 15\u00b0 C, 1 in-Hg/1,000 ft, 3.57\u00b0 C/1,000 ft", "30.00 in-Hg, 15\u00b0 C, 0.10 in-Hg/1,000 ft, 2\u00b0 C/1,000 ft"], a:0, r:"SG, The Standard Atmosphere: 29.92 in-Hg (1013.2 mb), 15\u00b0 C (59\u00b0 F), 1 in-Hg (34 mb) per 1,000 ft, 2\u00b0 C (3.57\u00b0 F) per 1,000 ft. B swaps 15\u00b0 C for 59 \u2014 but attaches the C unit to the Fahrenheit number. C uses the Fahrenheit lapse rate with a Celsius label. Each wrong answer contains exactly one unit or digit error.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.178"},
    {q:"For every ______ error by ______ . that the temperature varies from the standard atmosphere, the altimeter will be in", o:["4\u00b0 C; 11%", "2\u00b0 C; 4%", "11\u00b0 C; 4%", "11\u00b0 F; 4%"], a:2, r:"11\u00b0 C; 4%. SG, Temperature: \"For every 11 \u00b0C that the temperature varies from the standard, the altimeter will be in error by 4%. \" Choice A inverts the two numbers; choice D changes the unit to Fahrenheit.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.183"},
    {q:"An aircraft departs an area of high pressure and warm air and flies, without resetting the altimeter, into an area of both lower pressure and colder air. Relative to the indicated altitude, the aircraft will be:", o:["Higher than indicated, because both effects raise true altitude", "At the indicated altitude, because the two effects cancel", "Higher than indicated, because the pressure effect dominates the temperature effect", "Lower than indicated, because both effects lower true altitude"], a:3, r:"SG, Pressure and Temperature: \"High to Low, Look Out Below = The aircraft is actually at a lower altitude than indicated, \" and \"As you fly from warm to cold air, an altimeter will read too high\u2013the aircraft is lower than the altimeter indicates. \" Both errors act in the same direction here, so they compound rather than cancel. This is the pairing that kills aircraft in cold weather over mountains.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182 / 2.183"},
    {q:"A pilot departs NAS Pensacola (field elevation 30 ft MSL, altimeter setting 30.00 in-Hg) and flies to NAS Memphis (field elevation 300 ft MSL, altimeter setting 30.20 in-Hg) at an assigned indicated altitude of 1,000 feet, without resetting the altimeter. Over Memphis, the aircraft's AGL/absolute altitude is approximately:", o:["700 feet AGL", "1,000 feet AGL", "900 feet AGL", "1,200 feet AGL"], a:2, r:"900 feet AGL. Two steps. (1) Pressure change is low to high (30.00 \u2192 30.20), so \"Low to High, Plenty of Sky\" : altitude error = 0.20 \u00d7 1,000 = 200 ft, and MSL = assigned altitude + error = 1,000 + 200 = 1,200 ft MSL. (2) AGL = MSL \u2013 field elevation = 1,200 \u2013 300 = 900 ft AGL. Choice D (1,200 ft) is the correct MSL answer to a question that asked for AGL \u2014 read the altitude reference being requested.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"A pilot sets the altimeter correctly at Randolph AFB (29.85 in-Hg) and lands at Vance AFB (elevation 1,307 ft MSL, altimeter setting 30.15 in-Hg) without ever resetting the altimeter. On deck at Vance, the altimeter will indicate approximately:", o:["0 feet", "1,607 feet", "1,307 feet", "1,007 feet"], a:3, r:"1,007 feet. SG, Pressure: for a low-to-high change, \"Indicated Altitude on Deck = Field Elevation (\u2013) Altitude Error. \" Altitude error = (30.15 \u2013 29.85) \u00d7 1,000 = 300 ft, so 1,307 \u2013 300 = 1,007 ft. Note that in flight the aircraft was higher than indicated (Q11 of Test 2), yet on deck the altimeter reads lower than field elevation \u2014 same rule, applied to the correct formula.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.182"},
    {q:"Which one of the following statements about density altitude is correct?", o:["It is a height reference measured above the standard datum plane", "It is true altitude corrected for instrument error and nonstandard pressure", "It is pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature and serves as an index to aircraft performance rather than a height reference", "It is always higher than the published field elevation"], a:2, r:"SG, Density altitude: \"Density altitude (DA) is pressure altitude corrected for nonstandard temperature deviations\u2026 Density altitude is not a height reference; rather, it is an index to aircraft performance. \" Choice A defines pressure altitude. Choice D is false: on a cold day the DA is lower than field elevation.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.180"},
    {q:"A weather balloon measures a temperature profile that is constant with increasing altitude through a layer. This layer is best described as:", o:["An inversion, which is a stable layer", "The standard lapse rate, since 2\u00b0 C/1,000 ft is an average", "Isothermal, and part of the environmental lapse rate measured by the balloon", "Adiabatic, since no heat was added or removed"], a:2, r:"Isothermal, part of the environmental lapse rate. SG, Lapse Rates: \"An isothermal lapse rate indicates the temperature is the same at different altitudes, \" and \"The resulting temperature profile [from the weather balloon] is known as the environmental lapse rate. \" An inversion (A) requires temperature to increase with altitude. Adiabatic (D) describes a process, not a measured profile.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.176 / 2.294"},
    {q:"Air can reach saturation (RH = 100%) by which of the following?", o:["Cooling the air, which lowers the dew point toward the air temperature", "Evaporation only, since cooling does not change the dew point spread", "Warming the air, which increases its capacity to hold water vapor", "Cooling the air, which lowers the air temperature toward the dew point; or by evaporation, which raises the"], a:3, r:"SG, Atmospheric Moisture: \"If the air is cooled, the falling air temperature decreases the dew point spread closer to zero\u2026 If evaporation occurs, this adds moisture to the atmosphere, increasing the dew point, which again lowers the dew point spread. \" Choice A gets the mechanism backwards \u2014 cooling lowers the air temperature toward the dew point, not the dew point toward the air temperature.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.197 / 2.295"},
    {q:"Which one of the following statements about dew point is most correct?", o:["The dew point can be higher than, equal to, or lower than the air temperature depending on relative humidity", "The dew point is a direct indication of the amount of moisture present in the air, and can never be higher than the air temperature", "A higher dew point indicates a drier air mass and a lower chance of cloud formation", "The dew point depression is the difference between the dew point and the standard day temperature"], a:1, r:"SG, Atmospheric Moisture: \"The dew point is a direct indication of the amount of moisture present in the air\u2026 The dew point can never be higher than the air temperature: only equal to or less than. \" C reverses the meaning of a high dew point; D misdefines dew point depression (it is the spread between air temperature and dew point, not the standard day temperature).", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.198"},
    {q:"Air masses are named according to which three properties?", o:["Location, moisture content, and temperature", "Location, stability, and pressure", "Moisture content, temperature, and stability", "Latitude, altitude, and season"], a:0, r:"SG, Air Masses: \"Air masses are named according to their location, moisture content, and temperature. \" Stability is a consequence of temperature, not a naming category \u2014 which is what makes B and C tempting.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.184"},
    {q:"What is the primary reason atmospheric circulation takes place?", o:["The rotation of the Earth about its vertical axis", "The Coriolis force acting on air already in motion", "Ascending and descending currents caused by constant heating and cooling", "Magnetic variation between different regions of the Earth"], a:2, r:"SG, Atmospheric Circulation: \"Atmospheric circulation moves air in mainly two ways: ascending and descending currents. Because of the constant heating and cooling, the currents are always happening. \" Earth's rotation and the Coriolis force explain why the wind curves, not why circulation exists in the first place.", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.293"},
    {q:"Surface analysis charts reference all reported pressures to mean sea level rather than to station pressure because:", o:["Station pressure cannot be measured accurately at high-elevation stations", "Isobars can only be drawn at 4-millibar intervals at sea level", "Sea level pressure is the value set in the Kollsman window at all altitudes", "Station pressures would cause the chart to depict the inverse of land topography, with lows always over"], a:3, r:"SG, Station and Sea Level Pressure: \"If, instead, station pressures were used, the pressure charts would depict the inverse of the land topography\u2026 Mountain tops would always have lows over them, and valleys would have highs. \"", ref:"Topic 1, EO 2.179"},
    {q:"The wind at 3,000 feet AGL is from the north and is the result of a high pressure system. The surface wind in the same area would be from approximately the:", o:["Northeast", "Southwest", "Southeast", "Northwest"], a:3, r:"Northwest. Above 2,000 ft AGL the wind is a gradient wind flowing parallel to the isobars; at the surface, friction reduces the Coriolis force so the wind is turned back toward lower pressure by 45\u00b0 , i.e., outward from a high. A gradient wind from the north is moving toward the south; rotating that motion 45\u00b0 counterclockwise (outward from the high) points it toward the southeast, meaning it comes from the northwest. SG, Surface Winds: surface winds \"still move clockwise around highs\u2026 but since they blow across the isobars at a 45\u00b0 angle, they also have a component of motion that moves air out of the high pressure. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.186"},
    {q:"A station lies due south of the center of a low pressure system, above 2,000 feet AGL. The gradient wind at that station will be from approximately the:", o:["North", "South", "East", "West"], a:3, r:"From the west. Gradient winds flow counterclockwise around a low, parallel to the isobars. At a point due south of the low center, counterclockwise flow is directed toward the east \u2014 so the wind is coming from the west. Cross-check with Buys Ballot's Law: with the wind at your back (facing east), the low is to your left \u2014 due north \u2014 which is exactly where the low is. (Caution: a widely circulated legacy practice test keys this scenario \"from the east. \" That key contradicts both Buys Ballot's Law and the SG's own statement that circulation around a low is counterclockwise; the legacy explanation even says \"diverted to the right, so going West, \" which is not a right-hand deflection from a northward flow. Trust the SG.)", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.185"},
    {q:"Compared to summer, the wintertime jet stream is:", o:["Farther north, at a higher altitude, and slower", "Farther north, at a lower altitude, and faster", "Farther south, at a higher altitude, and slower", "Farther south, at a lower altitude, and faster"], a:3, r:"SG, The Jet Stream: \"During the winter, the position of the jet stream is further south, the core descends to lower altitudes, and its speed is faster than in the summer. \" All three attributes must be right \u2014 each wrong answer gets exactly one of them wrong.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.187"},
    {q:"A crew encounters jet stream turbulence. After beginning an altitude change they observe the outside air temperature rising. Which combination of actions is recommended?", o:["Turn north and descend", "Turn south and descend", "Turn south and climb", "Turn north and climb"], a:2, r:"Turn south and climb. SG, Jet Stream Turbulence: \"The horizontal shear on the cold air side of the core is stronger than on the warm air side. Thus, if it is desired to exit jet stream turbulence, a turn to the south should result in smoother air\u2026 If the temperature is rising, climb; if the temperature is falling, descend. \"", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.211 / 2.212"},
    {q:"A cloud with a base at 7,000 feet AGL and a top at 21,000 feet AGL is classified as a:", o:["Low cloud, because the base is below 10,000 feet", "Special cloud with extensive vertical development", "High cloud, because the top extends above 20,000 feet", "Middle cloud, because classification is determined by the height of the base"], a:3, r:"SG, Types of Clouds: \"The height of the cloud base, not the top, determines the classification. \" A base at 7,000 ft AGL falls in the 6,500\u201320,000 ft middle cloud band. Choice C is the trap for anyone who reads the top.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"Which statement about high clouds is correct?", o:["They pose a significant icing hazard because they contain super-cooled water droplets", "They produce steady precipitation but no turbulence", "They have little effect on flying except for moderate turbulence and limited visibility in dense jet stream cirrus, and are not an icing hazard", "They frequently hide terrain and produce low ceilings"], a:2, r:"SG, High Clouds: \"High clouds have little effect on flying except for moderate turbulence and limited visibility associated with dense jet stream cirrus. Since high clouds are composed mostly of ice crystals, they have no precipitation and do not constitute an icing hazard. \" (Severe/extreme turbulence is found in thunderstorm anvil cirrus, which is a separate case.)", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.196"},
    {q:"With respect to an occluded front, the most severe weather \u2014 including the lowest ceilings and visibilities \u2014 is generally located:", o:["100 NM north to 300 NM south of the frontal intersection", "500 to 700 NM east of the surface front", "Within 50 NM of the surface front in all directions", "100 NM south to 300 NM north of the frontal intersection"], a:3, r:"SG, Occluded Fronts: \"the most severe weather, including ceilings and visibilities, is generally located in an area 100 NM south to 300 NM north of the frontal intersection. \" Choice A reverses the two figures.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Which statement correctly distinguishes a warm front occlusion?", o:["The cold front remains in contact with the ground and undercuts the cool air ahead of the warm front", "It produces exclusively cold front type weather regardless of the direction of approach", "It is depicted in red on surface analysis charts and moves toward the southeast", "The air behind the cold front is not as cold as the cool air mass ahead of the warm front, so the cold air behind"], a:3, r:"SG, Occluded Fronts: \"Warm occlusions occur when the air behind the \u2018cold' front is actually not as cold as the cool air mass ahead of the warm front\u2026 the colder, denser air mass ahead of the warm front remains at the surface, forcing the cold air mass behind the cold front up as well. \" Choice A describes a cold occlusion. Choice C is wrong twice \u2014 occlusions are purple and move NE.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"During steady warm frontal rain, ceilings are often in which range, and precipitation typically begins where the middle cloud deck is at what height?", o:["300 to 900 feet; 8,000 to 12,000 feet AGL", "900 to 1,500 feet; 6,500 to 20,000 feet AGL", "300 to 900 feet; 500 to 700 feet AGL", "1,000 to 3,000 feet; 8,000 to 12,000 feet MSL"], a:0, r:"SG, Warm Front Flight Problems: \"Ceilings are often in the 300 to 900-foot range during steady, warm frontal rain situations, \" and \"precipitation will begin where the middle cloud deck is from 8000 to 12,000 feet AGL. \" Choice C substitutes the 500\u2013700 mile cloud shield extent as a height.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"If an active cold front is moving at a moderate to rapid speed (15\u201330 knots), its weather zone is generally:", o:["Less than 50 miles wide, with generally VMC conditions and isolated IMC in heavy precipitation and near thunderstorms", "Less than 50 miles wide, with widespread IMC conditions throughout", "50 to 300 miles wide, with generally VMC conditions", "500 to 700 miles wide, with low stratus and fog"], a:0, r:"SG, Cold Fronts Flight Problems: \"If an active cold front moves at a moderate or rapid speed (15-30 knots), its weather zone is generally less than 50 miles wide\u2026 Ceilings and visibilities are generally visual meteorological conditions (VMC), but isolated instrument meteorological conditions (IMC) exist in heavy precipitation and near thunderstorms. \" B has the right width but the wrong flight conditions.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"A pilot flying through a front notes that temperature, dew point, and wind change abruptly over a very short distance. This indicates:", o:["A broad, diffuse frontal zone often over 200 miles wide, with generally milder weather", "That the aircraft has crossed the upper front rather than the surface front", "A stationary front, since only stationary fronts produce abrupt changes", "A narrow frontal zone, in some cases less than a mile wide, associated with more severe weather"], a:3, r:"SG, Frontal Systems: \"Abrupt changes indicate that the frontal zone is narrow, and in some cases, the zone can be less than a mile wide\u2026 Abrupt changes will bring more severe weather than gradual changes. \" Choice A describes the opposite case (gradual changes, broad zone over 200 miles).", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.190 / 2.191"},
    {q:"At approximately what altitude do the differences between the two air masses forming a front generally become negligible?", o:["Above 6,500 to 10,000 feet", "Above 15,000 to 20,000 feet", "Above 25,000 to 30,000 feet", "At the tropopause, in all cases"], a:1, r:"SG, General Frontal Structure: \"At some level above the surface, usually above 15,000 to 20,000 feet, the differences between the two air masses forming the front become negligible. \" Choice D is wrong as an absolute, though the SG notes the temperature contrast can sometimes extend as high as the tropopause.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.190"},
    {q:"Turbulence along a warm front is best described by which statement?", o:["Turbulence is widespread throughout the warm frontal cloud shield due to the shallow slope", "The only turbulence normally found along a warm front is in embedded thunderstorms; otherwise little to no turbulence exists", "Warm front turbulence is generally more severe than cold front turbulence because of the greater areal extent", "Turbulence is caused by the abrupt 90\u00b0 wind shift, which is more sudden than that of a cold front"], a:1, r:"SG, Turbulence and Thunderstorms (warm front): \"The only turbulence along a warm front would be found in such embedded thunderstorms. Otherwise, little to no turbulence exists in warm front systems. \" Choice D is doubly wrong \u2014 the warm front wind shift is less sudden than a cold front's, and it is a 90\u00b0 shift from SE to SW.", ref:"Topic 2, EO 2.192"},
    {q:"Which one of the following correctly states the extent of thunderstorm-related turbulence?", o:["Turbulence can extend over 5,000 feet above the cloud tops and 20 miles laterally, with severe turbulence in the anvil 15 to 30 miles downwind", "Turbulence can extend over 1,000 feet above the cloud tops and 5 miles laterally, with severe turbulence in the anvil 5 to 10 miles downwind", "Turbulence is confined to the visible cloud and the area directly beneath the cloud base", "Turbulence extends 5,000 feet above the tops but only within the precipitation shaft"], a:0, r:"SG, Turbulence Associated with Thunderstorms: \"wind shear turbulence has been encountered several thousand feet above and 20 miles laterally from a severe storm. Severe turbulence can be encountered in the anvil 15 to 30 miles downwind, \" and Extreme Turbulence: \"This turbulence can extend over 5000 feet above the cloud tops and down to the ground beneath the cloud base. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Which one of the following radar separation rules is stated correctly?", o:["Avoid severe or intense echoes by at least 20 miles; echoes should be separated by at least 40 miles before flying between them; severe CAT and hail may be experienced between storms if echo separation is less than 30 miles", "Avoid severe echoes by at least 40 miles; echoes should be separated by at least 20 miles before flying between them; severe CAT may occur if separation is less than 20 miles", "Avoid severe echoes by at least 10 miles; echoes should be separated by at least 30 miles before flying between them", "Avoid severe echoes by at least 20 miles; echoes should be separated by at least 30 miles; hail is not a concern"], a:0, r:"SG, Flight Techniques in the Vicinity of Thunderstorms, items 14 and 3: \"Avoid by at least 20 mi any thunderstorm identified as severe\u2026 Such echoes should be separated by at least 40 mi before flying between echoes, \" and \"Severe clear air turbulence and hail may be experienced between thunderstorms if the separation between echoes is less than 30 miles. \" Three different numbers \u2014 20, 40, 30 \u2014 each attached to a different rule. B swaps the 20 and 40.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.225"},
    {q:"A thunderstorm with radar echo tops indicated above hail and should be regarded as extremely hazardous. often contains extreme turbulence and ______", o:["25,000 feet", "30,000 feet", "35,000 feet", "45,000 feet"], a:2, r:"35,000 feet. SG, RADAR information: \"A thunderstorm with radar echo tops indicated above 35,000 feet often contains extreme turbulence and hail, \" and item 17: \"Regard as extremely hazardous any thunderstorm with tops 35,000 ft or higher. \" (45,000 ft is the altitude at which hail has been encountered in clear air.)", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"A thunderstorm cell is considered to have entered the mature stage when:", o:["The updraft exceeds 3,000 feet per minute", "The anvil first becomes visible", "Precipitation reaches the surface", "Subsiding air replaces the updraft throughout the cloud"], a:2, r:"SG, Mature Stage: \"The cell transitions to the mature stage when precipitation reaches the surface. \" A describes the towering cumulus stage; D describes the dissipating stage; B is explicitly unreliable \u2014 \"the appearance of an anvil does not indicate the thunderstorm is free of hazards. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Which set of microburst parameters is stated correctly?", o:["Downward velocities of 2,000 to over 6,000 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 200 knots; diameter of \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles; life span of 5 to 10 minutes after reaching the ground", "Downward velocities of 2,000 to over 6,000 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 50 knots; diameter of 2 to 5 miles; life span of 20 minutes to 1\u00bd hours", "Downward velocities of 200 to 600 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 200 knots; diameter of \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles; life span of 5 to 10 minutes", "Downward velocities of 2,000 to over 6,000 fpm; differential velocities of 20 to 200 knots; diameter of 5 to 10"], a:0, r:"SG, Microbursts: \"velocities of 2000 to over 6000 feet per minute\u2026 producing a vortex ring of wind that can produce differential velocities ranging from 20 to 200 knots in an area only \u00bc to 2\u00bd miles in diameter\u2026 its short life span\u2013usually only 5 to 10 minutes after reaching the ground. \" Choice B substitutes the thunderstorm cell life cycle (20 min to 1\u00bd hr).", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"An aircraft on final approach enters a microburst and the crew observes an increasing headwind, rising indicated airspeed, and a pitch-up tendency. The correct response is to:", o:["Reduce power and apply nose-down stick to hold the glidepath, since the airspeed excess will dissipate", "Extend additional drag devices to bleed off the excess airspeed", "Maintain the current power setting and allow the aircraft to stabilize on its own", "Anticipate the imminent transition to a strong tailwind and downdraft, and add maximum power while"], a:3, r:"SG, Microbursts (Figure 3\u201121): the pilot's natural reaction of reducing power and pushing the nose down \"would correct the situation if the aircraft was not in a microburst, \" but at position 3\u20134 the headwind becomes a strong tailwind in a severe downdraft, and \"the correct reaction would be to add maximum power and establish a climbing attitude on the vertical gyro. \" Choice A is the instinctive \u2014 and fatal \u2014 response.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.224"},
    {q:"Most aircraft lightning strikes occur under which conditions?", o:["Within 8\u00b0 C of the freezing level, within approximately 5,000 feet of the freezing level, in precipitation, in clouds, and in some turbulence", "Within 8\u00b0 C of the freezing level, within approximately 1,000 feet of the freezing level, and only inside cumulonimbus clouds", "Above 43,000 feet, in clear air, and outside of precipitation", "Only within the anvil, and only while the storm is in the mature stage"], a:0, r:"SG, Aircraft Lightning or ESD Encounters: strikes occur \"1. Within 8\u00b0 C of the freezing level; 2. Within approximately 5000 feet of the freezing level; 3. In precipitation, including snow; 4. In clouds; 5. In some turbulence. \" Note the SG's caveat that not all of these need occur \u2014 but 5,000 ft, not 1,000 ft, is the stated figure. Choice C misuses the 43,000-foot figure, which is the highest altitude at which strikes have been recorded.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.223"},
    {q:"Which statement about icing and cloud temperature is correct?", o:["Icing is impossible below \u201320\u00b0 C because all clouds are ice crystals at that temperature", "Serious icing is rare in clouds colder than \u201320\u00b0 C since these clouds are almost completely composed of ice crystals, but icing is possible in any cloud when the temperature is 0\u00b0 C or below", "Water droplets in free air freeze at 0\u00b0 C, so icing is certain in any cloud below freezing", "The larger the droplet, the lower its freezing point"], a:1, r:"SG, Icing Requirements: \"As a general rule, serious icing is rare in clouds with temperatures below \u201320\u00b0 C since these clouds are almost completely composed of ice crystals. However, be aware that icing is possible in any cloud when the temperature is 0\u00b0 C or below. \" Choice D is backwards: \"the smaller the droplets, the lower the freezing point. \"", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.214 / 2.215"},
    {q:"Compressor icing is indicated to the pilot by:", o:["A loss of thrust and a rapid rise in exhaust gas temperature", "A loss of thrust and a rapid drop in exhaust gas temperature", "An increase in thrust and a decrease in the fuel-air ratio", "Erroneous airspeed indications with no change in engine parameters"], a:0, r:"SG, Compressor icing: \"The reduction in airflow is noticeable through a loss of thrust and a rapid rise in exhaust gas temperature. As the airflow decreases, the fuel-air ratio increases. \" Choices B and C each invert one of these two indications.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.217"},
    {q:"Regarding the aerodynamic effect of ice on an airfoil, which statement is correct?", o:["Ice thickness is the only significant factor in determining the loss of lift", "Distributed sandpaper-like roughness on the leading edge has a negligible effect on lift", "A half-inch ridge of ice at 50% chord reduces maximum lift by over 50%, while the same ridge at 4% chord reduces it by only about 15%", "A half-inch ridge of ice at 4% chord reduces maximum lift by over 50%, while the same ridge at 50% chord"], a:3, r:"SG, Effects and Hazards of Structural Icing: \"a half-inch high ridge of ice on the upper surface of the airfoil at 4% chord reduces maximum lift by over 50%. Yet, the same ridge of ice at 50% chord decreases maximum lift by only 15%. \" The SG explicitly states that \"Ice thickness is not the only factor\u2026 Location, roughness, and shape are important, too, \" and that leading-edge roughness alone may cost 35% of lift.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.216"},
    {q:"The primary distinction between fog and stratus is that:", o:["Fog is composed of water droplets while stratus is composed only of ice crystals", "Stratus is not surface based \u2014 it is above 50 feet AGL and does not reduce horizontal visibility at the surface", "Fog reduces vertical visibility only, while stratus reduces horizontal visibility only", "Stratus always constitutes a ceiling while fog never does"], a:1, r:"SG, Fog vs Stratus: \"The main distinction between fog and stratus is that a stratus layer is not surface based. It is above the ground (greater than 50 feet AGL) and does not reduce the horizontal visibility at the surface. \" Both are composed of the same small water droplets or ice crystals, so A is false.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.219 / 2.220"},
    {q:"Advection fog dissipates:", o:["Primarily through solar heating shortly after sunrise", "Only with a change in wind direction that moves the air from a colder surface to a warmer surface", "When winds decrease below 5 knots", "When the dew point spread increases due to nocturnal cooling"], a:1, r:"SG, Advection Fog: \"Advection fog dissipates only with a wind shift\u2026 Incoming solar radiation will seldom cause the dissipation of advection fog\u2026 Only a change in wind direction that moves the air from a colder surface to a warmer surface, reversing the saturation process, can cause advection fog to dissipate. \" Choice A is how radiation fog dissipates. Choice C is backwards \u2014 advection fog gets thicker as wind increases up to about 15 knots.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.222"},
    {q:"Fog that extends no more than 200 feet in height is considered shallow fog. One of the most serious problems it presents on approach is that:", o:["It normally causes a total obscuration, so no visual cues appear at any point", "There is an abundance of visual cues early in the approach, and the subsequent loss of those cues upon entering the fog layer may cause confusion or disorientation", "It reduces vertical visibility to zero while leaving slant range visibility unaffected", "It is always reported as a total obscuration with a vertical visibility group"], a:1, r:"SG, Fog vs Stratus: \"One of the most serious problems with shallow fog stems from the abundance of cues available at the start of the approach\u2026 as the fog level is entered, loss of visual cues may cause confusion or disorientation. \" Choice A describes dense fog (total obscuration); shallow fog \"is normally reported as a partial obscuration, \" so D is also wrong.", ref:"Topic 3, EO 2.219"},
    {q:"An observer can see 3 miles throughout 200\u00b0 of the horizon circle, but a bank of fog to the east limits visibility to \u00bd mile over the remaining 160\u00b0 . The prevailing visibility is:", o:["\u00bd mile", "1\u00be miles, the average of the two sectors", "3 miles", "Not reportable, since the horizon circle is not continuous"], a:2, r:"3 miles. SG, Visibility Definitions: prevailing visibility is \"the greatest horizontal visibility\u2026 equaled or exceeded throughout at least half the horizon circle, which need not be continuous. \" 200\u00b0 exceeds 180\u00b0 , so 3 miles is the prevailing visibility even though a large sector is much worse. Prevailing visibility is never an average (B), and the SG's Figure 4\u20111 uses exactly this fog-bank scenario.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.226"},
    {q:"A reported vertical visibility of 200 feet differs from a reported cloud ceiling of 200 feet because:", o:["Vertical visibility is measured in feet MSL while a ceiling is measured in feet AGL", "With a cloud ceiling, a pilot can normally expect to see the ground and runway upon descending below the base; with vertical visibility, the obscuring phenomenon also degrades slant range visibility, so the runway may still be difficult to see", "Vertical visibility is reported only for partial obscurations, which are not ceilings", "A vertical visibility of 200 feet indicates better visual conditions than a 200-foot cloud ceiling"], a:1, r:"SG, Sky Coverage and Ceilings: \"With a low cloud ceiling, a pilot normally can expect to see the ground and the runway once the aircraft descends below the cloud base. However, in the case of vertical visibility, the obscuring phenomenon also reduces the slant range visibility. Therefore, a pilot will have difficulty seeing the runway or approach lights clearly even after descending below the level of the reported vertical visibility. \" Both are reported in feet AGL, so A is false; a vertical visibility report is a total obscuration and does constitute a ceiling, so C is false.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.227"},
    {q:"A TAF contains the icing group 641104. This forecasts:", o:["Moderate icing from 11,000 feet AGL to 15,000 feet AGL", "Severe icing from 11,000 feet AGL to 14,000 feet AGL", "Moderate icing from 1,100 feet AGL to 5,100 feet AGL", "Moderate icing from 11,000 feet MSL, thickness unspecified"], a:0, r:"SG, Icing Group: 6=icing forecast; 4=moderate icing; 110=base at 11,000 ft AGL; 4=layer 4,000 ft thick. Base 11,000 + thickness 4,000 = tops at 15,000 ft AGL. Choice B misreads the intensity digit; choice C misplaces the decimal on the base height.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"A TAF contains the group 530004. This forecasts:", o:["Light turbulence from the surface to 4,000 feet AGL", "Moderate turbulence in clear air from the surface to 4,000 feet AGL", "Severe icing from the surface to 4,000 feet AGL", "Moderate turbulence from 3,000 feet to 4,000 feet AGL"], a:1, r:"SG, Turbulence Group: 5=turbulence forecast; 3=moderate turbulence in clear air (per Table 4\u20117); 000=base at or below 100 ft AGL (surface); 4=4,000 ft thick. The SG decodes this exact group in its TAF example as \"moderate turbulence in clear air from surface up to 4000 feet. \" A group beginning with 6 would be icing, so C is a category error.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"On a Winds-Aloft Forecast, the code 7409 decodes to:", o:["740\u00b0 at 09 knots \u2014 a coding error", "240\u00b0 at 109 knots", "740\u00b0 at 109 knots", "240\u00b0 at 09 knots"], a:1, r:"240\u00b0 T at 109 knots. SG, Special Circumstances 3: for winds of 100 knots or greater, 500 is added to the direction and 100 subtracted from the speed. To decode, subtract 500 from the \"unrealistic\" direction (740 \u2013 500 = 240) and add 100 to the speed (09 + 100 = 109). The SG uses 7409 as its own worked example.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"Which one of the following correctly states the Winds-Aloft Forecast omission rules?", o:["Wind is never forecast within 1,500 feet of the surface; temperature is never forecast within 2,500 feet of the surface; temperature is never forecast for the 3,000-foot level", "Wind is never forecast within 2,500 feet of the surface; temperature is never forecast within 1,500 feet of the surface", "Wind and temperature are both omitted within 3,000 feet of the surface", "Temperature is omitted above 24,000 feet, since all temperatures are negative there"], a:0, r:"SG, Special Circumstances 4: \"Wind information is never forecast for altitudes within 1500 feet of the surface. Temperature information is never forecast within 2500 feet of the surface. Temperature information is never forecast for the 3000-foot level. \" Choice B swaps the two figures; choice D inverts the rule \u2014 above 24,000 ft temperatures are forecast, and are simply all negative.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.27"},
    {q:"Which statement about Convective SIGMETs is correct?", o:["They are issued every 6 hours and are valid for 6 hours", "They are issued hourly at H+55, the forecast is valid for up to 2 hours, and any Convective SIGMET implies severe or greater turbulence, severe icing, and low-level wind shear", "They are issued hourly and imply only moderate turbulence and moderate icing", "They are issued only when a tornado has been observed"], a:1, r:"SG, US Convective SIGMETs: \"Bulletins are issued hourly at Hour+55\u2026 The forecast is valid for up to 2 hours, \" and \"Any convective SIGMET implies severe or greater turbulence, severe icing, and low level wind shear. \" Choice A gives the non-convective SIGMET timing (4 hours, or 6 for hurricanes).", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.229"},
    {q:"A G-AIRMET depicting areas of moderate airframe icing and the freezing level is designated:", o:["SIERRA", "TANGO", "ZULU", "Convective SIGMET"], a:2, r:"ZULU. SG, G-AIRMET: \"Icing and Freezing Level AIRMETs are ZULU AIRMETs. \" TANGO covers turbulence, LLWS, and strong surface winds; SIERRA covers IFR conditions and mountain obscuration.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 2.229"},
    {q:"Squalls (SQ) are reported in the present weather section of a METAR when there is:", o:["A sudden increase in wind speed of at least 16 knots, sustained at 22 knots or more, for at least 1 minute", "A variation of 10 knots or more between peaks and lulls", "A change in wind direction of 45 degrees or more in less than 15 minutes", "Any wind gust exceeding 25 knots since the last METAR"], a:0, r:"SG, Group 4: Wind, Note 2: \"A sudden increase in wind speed of at least 16 knots and sustained at 22 knots or more for at least 1 minute requires that Squalls (SQ) be reported. \" Choice B defines a gust; choice C defines a wind shift; choice D describes peak wind.", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.23"},
    {q:"A BECMG group in a TAF indicates a change occurring over a period that is normally about ______ and at most . ______", o:["2 hours; 4 hours", "1 hour; 2 hours", "4 hours; 8 hours", "Instantaneous; 1 hour"], a:0, r:"about 2 hours, at most 4. SG, BECMG Group: \"The duration of this change is normally about 2 hours, 4 hours at most and is how long the weather depicted within the BECMG line will take to fully develop. \"", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.25"},
    {q:"When using data-linked NEXRAD mosaic imagery in flight, a pilot must remember that:", o:["The imagery is real time and may be used as the sole means of tactical maneuvering through a thunderstorm area", "The imagery shows where the weather was, not where it is, and conditions may be 15 to 20 minutes older than the age indicated on the display", "The imagery reliably detects microbursts out to 100 NM from the radar site", "Echoes rarely change shape or intensity in less than one hour"], a:1, r:"SG, Flight Techniques in the Vicinity of Thunderstorms, item 8: \"Remember that the data-linked NEXRAD mosaic imagery shows where the weather was, not where the weather is. The weather conditions may be 15 to 20 minutes older than the age indicated on the display. \" Choice A contradicts the SG's guidance on tactical maneuvering; choice C misstates the microburst detection limit, which is about 20 NM from the radar site; choice D contradicts the SG's warning that echoes \"can change shape, character, and intensity in a matter of minutes. \" APPENDIX \u2014 LEGACY PRACTICE-TEST ITEMS EXCLUDED AFTER CROSS-CHECK The following items appear in the older practice tests supplied but are not supported by the January 2025 Student Guide and were deliberately left out of all three tests: Legacy item Tropical disturbance / tropical cyclone wind thresholds; Gale and Storm Warning Conditions I\u2013III Frontogenesis / frontolysis Air mass letter codes (mTk, cPw, mPk, cTk, etc.) \"Water vapor is 0 to 10% of the atmosphere by volume\" Middle clouds \"5,500\u201318,500 ft\" ; high clouds \"above 18,500 ft\" \"Fog base at or lower than 50 feet\u2026 (True)\" as a standalone T/F Why it was excluded No tropical cyclone or warning-condition material appears anywhere in the Jan 2025 SG. Neither term appears in the Jan 2025 SG. The Jan 2025 SG names air masses by location, moisture content, and temperature but does not teach the letter-code shorthand. Jan 2025 SG states 0% to 5% by volume. Jan 2025 SG uses 6,500\u201320,000 ft AGL (middle) and above 20,000 ft AGL (high). Jan 2025 SG requires all three parameters: based at or within 50 ft of the surface, greater than 20 ft in depth, and prevailing visibility less than \u215d SM. The legacy T/F omits the depth and visibility criteria. \"Most severe icing between +2\u00b0 C and \u201310\u00b0 C\" Station pressure problem keyed as 1130.0 mb Gradient wind at a station south of a low keyed as \"from the east\" \"Five factors that influence frontal weather\" (slope, stability, moisture, speed, contrast) as a numbered list T-34C / carburetor-icing-specific engine questions Turbulence intensity \"Trace\" End of Test 3. Jan 2025 SG states clear icing occurs 0\u00b0 C to \u201310\u00b0 C; rime \u201310 to \u201320; mixed \u20138 to \u201315. No \"+2\u00b0 C\" figure exists. Physically and doctrinally wrong \u2014 station pressure must be lower than sea level pressure. Correct answer is 960.0 mb (used as Test 2, Q6). Contradicts counterclockwise circulation around a low and Buys Ballot's Law. Correct answer is from the west (used as Test 3, Q15). The Jan 2025 SG discusses slope, speed, moisture, and contrast, but does not present them as an enumerated set of five, so a \"which two of the five\" question is not testable as written. The Jan 2025 SG discusses induction and compressor icing generically and does not tie engine icing to a specific airframe. Trace is an icing intensity. Turbulence intensities are light, moderate, severe, extreme. (Retained, but only as a distractor \u2014 see Test 1, Q28.)", ref:"Topic 4, EO 4.28"},
  ]
},
];
